1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
Hi. In this video, we'll look at testing for WebMessage DOM XSS with DOMInvader.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,840
WebMessage DOM XSS occurs if the destination origin for a web message trusts the sender

3
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:23,760
not to transmit malicious data in the message, and handles the data in an unsafe way by passing

4
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:26,400
it into a sync.

5
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:31,160
You can use DOMInvader to test applications for WebMessage DOM XSS.

6
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:36,240
DOMInvader enables you to log any messages that are sent via the post message method, and

7
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:40,040
modify and resend web messages.

8
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:43,880
You can learn more about sources and syncs in the DOM-based vulnerabilities section on

9
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:46,880
our Web Security Academy.

10
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:52,080
If you'd like to follow along with these steps, you can use the lab DOM XSS using WebMessages,

11
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:56,840
which is also available in our Web Security Academy.

12
00:00:56,840 --> 00:00:59,840
DOMInvader is pre-installed in Burps Browser.

13
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:05,240
It's disabled by default as some of its features may interfere with your other testing activities.

14
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:10,840
So before we start, we'll need to enable DOMInvader, including post message interception.

15
00:01:10,840 --> 00:01:15,600
Use Burps Browser to visit your target website.

16
00:01:15,600 --> 00:01:21,080
Right click the browser window and select Inspect.

17
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:25,040
Go to the DOMInvader tab and select Messages from the right hand panel.

18
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:29,720
This will display the messages that DOMInvader has flagged as exploitable.

19
00:01:29,720 --> 00:01:33,840
Click each message to review it and see if the origin, data or source properties of the

20
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:38,480
message are accessed by the client side JavaScript.

21
00:01:38,480 --> 00:01:43,840
If the origin property isn't accessed, it's likely that the origin isn't being validated.

22
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:48,560
If the data property isn't accessed, the message can't be exploited.

23
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:52,900
If the source property isn't accessed, it's likely the source, usually in iframe, isn't

24
00:01:52,900 --> 00:01:56,120
being validated.

25
00:01:56,120 --> 00:02:00,300
You can use the message information to craft an exploit and then use DOMInvader to send a

26
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:02,440
modified web message.

27
00:02:02,440 --> 00:02:07,440
To do this, review the message information to identify the type of sync the data ends up

28
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:08,440
in.

29
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:18,800
Then edit the data field with an exploit that matches the sync type.

30
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:29,180
Click Send.

31
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:35,460
If you find an exploitable vulnerability, you can use DOMInvader to generate a proof of concept.

32
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:40,500
Simply select the vulnerable message to open the message details dialog, modify the values

33
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:47,180
as required for your exploit, and then click on Build POC to save the HTML to your clipboard.

34
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:48,180
!

